{"reply":"I wouldn’t make that leap. In Chapter 5, *Advice from a Caterpillar*, Alice is asked, “Who are _you?_” and answers, “I—I hardly know, sir, just at present—” Her difficulty makes room for seeing identity as changing and context-sensitive, but it does not decide which of your voices is the real one.

Your earlier note reports a continuity across the settings: you held the same view about the timetable, while your participation changed—you led the meeting and listened more at dinner. Hume’s *Of personal identity* argues that introspection finds changing perceptions rather than one constant self, and that we often mistake a connected succession for a perfectly unchanging identity ([Hume, “Of personal identity”](https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6)).

Taken together, these accounts challenge the demand for one uniform presentation, but they do not establish the cause of your changes or prove that either voice is an act. One possibility is that both are genuine ways of participating in different relationships; you might ask what remains continuous across them—your judgment, your values, your comfort, or something else?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","supported_claims":["In Chapter 5, *Advice from a Caterpillar*, Alice is asked, “Who are _you?_” and answers, “I—I hardly know, sir, just at present—” Her difficulty makes room for seeing identity as changing and context-sensitive, but it does not decide which of your voices is the real one."],"exact_quote":"“I—I hardly know, sir, just at present—"},{"source_kind":"memory","evidence_id":"mem_fce84ad964d588025db33292c91c6ec9c3070b04239cdf68227d042aec28040e","supported_claims":["Your earlier note reports a continuity across the settings: you held the same view about the timetable, while your participation changed—you led the meeting and listened more at dinner.","One possibility is that both are genuine ways of participating in different relationships"],"exact_quote":null},{"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://davidhume.org/texts/t/1/4/6","supported_claims":["Hume’s *Of personal identity* argues that introspection finds changing perceptions rather than one constant self, and that we often mistake a connected succession for a perfectly unchanging identity","Taken together, these accounts challenge the demand for one uniform presentation, but they do not establish the cause of your changes or prove that either voice is an act."],"exact_quote":null}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

